ind a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399215 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399215 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399216 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1